{"meta": {"course": "bio-inspired", "chapter": "06", "section": "01", "title": "Module 6: Thermal Regulation & Architecture", "description": "Analyze how termite mounds use thermal mass and pressure-driven airflow, then translate those principles into passive architectural cooling strategies.", "order": 6, "chapter_title": "Thermal Regulation and Environmental Control", "learning_objectives": ["Explain how the stack effect contributes to passive thermal regulation.", "Identify the roles of vent geometry, thermal mass, and distributed airflow in termite mounds.", "Translate termite-inspired regulation principles into building or engineering design heuristics."], "concept_ids": ["stack_effect", "thermal_mass", "passive_cooling", "biomimetic_architecture"], "estimated_time_minutes": 13, "prereq_section_ids": ["bio-inspired/05/01"], "practice_ids": ["bio_stack_effect_core", "bio_thermal_mass_role", "bio_passive_cooling_transfer", "bio_architecture_environment_fit", "bio_problem_to_biology_translation", "bio_asknature_entry_logic"]}, "content": "# Module 6: Thermal Regulation and Architecture\n\n> **\"The termite does not air-condition its mound. It shapes the mound so the air conditions itself.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Explain** how the stack effect drives passive thermal regulation, and quantify the pressure it produces.\n2. **Identify** the roles of vent geometry, thermal mass, and distributed airflow in termite mounds.\n3. **Calculate** buoyancy-driven airflow and thermal-mass storage for a simple passive design.\n4. **Translate** termite-inspired regulation principles into building and engineering design heuristics.\n\n---\n\n## Why Termite Mounds Matter to Engineers\n\nThe African termite mound is one of the cleanest examples of **environmental control without conventional machinery**. In the open savanna, outside temperature can swing from intense daytime heat (often above $40^\\circ\\text{C}$) to cold night air near $15^\\circ\\text{C}$. Inside the mound, however, the colony keeps conditions near the narrow band needed for fungus cultivation, moisture balance, and survival, frequently holding the core within a few degrees of $31^\\circ\\text{C}$.\n\nFor an engineer, the mound is not just a nest. It is a **distributed thermal regulation system** that handles airflow, heat transfer, humidity control, and carbon dioxide exchange at once, driven by temperature differences of only a handful of degrees.\n\nThree design principles make the mound powerful:\n\n1. **Thermal buffering:** The mound walls and surrounding soil absorb heat slowly, which reduces sudden temperature spikes.\n2. **Pressure-driven circulation:** Small pressure differences between the warm interior and cooler exterior create continuous airflow.\n3. **Distributed vents instead of one big machine:** The mound works because many small pathways share the load of exchange.\n\nThis matters because human buildings often attack cooling with large, centralized equipment. Termites show a different logic: shape the structure so that the environment does part of the work for you.\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"thermodynamics\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## The Stack Effect, Made Quantitative\n\nThe most important physical idea here is the **stack effect**, sometimes called the chimney effect. Warm air is less dense than cool air, so it naturally rises. If a structure gives that warm air a path upward, the rising column creates a pressure difference that pulls cooler air in somewhere lower.\n\n### The Mechanism in Plain Language\n\n- Metabolic activity from termites and the cultivated fungus produces heat.\n- Slightly warmer air rises through internal shafts.\n- Cooler outside air is drawn in through lower pores and channels.\n- The moving air regulates both temperature and gas exchange together.\n\nThe key insight is that the mound does not need a smart thermostat in the modern sense. Its **geometry acts like a control system**.\n\n<inline-check question=\"In the stack effect, what actually pulls cooler outside air in through the lower openings?\" explanation=\"The rising warm air column is less dense than the outside air. That density difference creates a small low-pressure region near the base, and the higher-pressure cool air outside flows in to replace what rose and left at the top. No fan or active pump is involved.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A fan hidden in the mound walls\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The pressure difference created by the rising, less-dense warm air column\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Termites physically pushing air with their bodies\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Wind pressure alone, independent of temperature\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n### The Governing Equation\n\nThe driving pressure from the stack effect depends on the height of the air column and on how much warmer the inside air is than the outside air:\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\Delta P = \\rho_o \\, g \\, h \\, \\frac{T_i - T_o}{T_i}}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\rho_o$ is the density of the cooler outside air (kg/m$^3$), $g = 9.81\\ \\text{m/s}^2$, $h$ is the effective vertical distance between the low intake and the high exhaust (m), and $T_i$, $T_o$ are the inside and outside **absolute** temperatures (Kelvin). The temperature difference appears as a ratio because what actually drives the flow is the **density difference** between the two air columns. Even a $7^\\circ\\text{C}$ gap between $25^\\circ\\text{C}$ and $32^\\circ\\text{C}$ air changes density by only about $0.027\\ \\text{kg/m}^3$ (from roughly $1.184$ to $1.157\\ \\text{kg/m}^3$), yet over enough height that small difference produces usable airflow.\n\nOnce $\\Delta P$ is known, the airflow speed through a vent follows from a Bernoulli-type orifice relation:\n\n$$\nv = C_d \\sqrt{\\frac{2\\,\\Delta P}{\\rho}}, \\qquad Q = v \\, A\n$$\n\nHere $C_d$ is a discharge coefficient (about $0.6$ to $0.65$ for a sharp opening), $A$ is the open vent area, and $Q$ is the volumetric flow rate (m$^3$/s).\n\nUse the explorer below to feel how stack pressure, vent speed, and volumetric flow respond to the design variables. Notice how a small temperature gap, applied over a tall shaft, can drive real airflow with no fan.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Stack-Effect Ventilation Designer\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"h\",\"label\":\"Effective stack height\",\"min\":1,\"max\":12,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":4},{\"name\":\"Ti\",\"label\":\"Inside temperature\",\"min\":300,\"max\":315,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"K\",\"default\":305.15},{\"name\":\"To\",\"label\":\"Outside (intake) temperature\",\"min\":285,\"max\":305,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"K\",\"default\":298.15},{\"name\":\"A\",\"label\":\"Open vent area\",\"min\":0.1,\"max\":2,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"m^2\",\"default\":0.5}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Stack pressure dP\",\"unit\":\"Pa\",\"expr\":\"1.184 * 9.81 * h * (Ti - To) / Ti\"},{\"label\":\"Vent speed v\",\"unit\":\"m/s\",\"expr\":\"0.65 * sqrt(2 * (1.184 * 9.81 * h * (Ti - To) / Ti) / 1.184)\"},{\"label\":\"Volumetric flow Q\",\"unit\":\"m^3/s\",\"expr\":\"A * 0.65 * sqrt(2 * (1.184 * 9.81 * h * (Ti - To) / Ti) / 1.184)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"h\",\"output\":0}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n### What Engineers Should Notice\n\n- Airflow is shaped by section size, height, and resistance: doubling $h$ doubles $\\Delta P$.\n- Vent placement matters as much as vent size; intake low and exhaust high maximizes the effective $h$.\n- Thermal mass delays heat movement, buying time for passive regulation.\n- A good passive system balances temperature, humidity, and air quality together rather than optimizing just one variable.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 1: Buoyancy-Driven Ventilation\n\n**Problem.** A termite-inspired ventilation shaft has an effective stack height of $h = 4\\ \\text{m}$. The interior air sits at $T_i = 32^\\circ\\text{C}$ while cooler intake air is at $T_o = 25^\\circ\\text{C}$. The exhaust vent has an open area of $A = 0.5\\ \\text{m}^2$ with a discharge coefficient $C_d = 0.65$. Outside air density is $\\rho_o = 1.184\\ \\text{kg/m}^3$. Estimate the stack pressure, the airflow speed, and the volumetric flow rate. If the protected space has a volume of $300\\ \\text{m}^3$, how many air changes per hour (ACH) does this provide?\n\n**Step 1: Convert temperatures to Kelvin.**\n\n$$\nT_i = 32 + 273.15 = 305.15\\ \\text{K}, \\qquad T_o = 25 + 273.15 = 298.15\\ \\text{K}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Compute the stack pressure.**\n\n$$\n\\Delta P = (1.184)(9.81)(4)\\,\\frac{305.15 - 298.15}{305.15}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\Delta P = 46.46 \\times \\frac{7}{305.15} = 46.46 \\times 0.02294 = \\boxed{1.07\\ \\text{Pa}}\n$$\n\nA single pascal sounds tiny, but it is enough to move air steadily through a low-resistance path.\n\n**Step 3: Compute the airflow speed.**\n\n$$\nv = 0.65 \\sqrt{\\frac{2(1.07)}{1.184}} = 0.65 \\sqrt{1.808} = 0.65 (1.345) = \\boxed{0.87\\ \\text{m/s}}\n$$\n\n**Step 4: Compute the volumetric flow and air changes.**\n\n$$\nQ = vA = (0.87)(0.5) = 0.436\\ \\text{m}^3/\\text{s} = 1570\\ \\text{m}^3/\\text{h}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\text{ACH} = \\frac{Q}{V} = \\frac{1570}{300} = \\boxed{5.2\\ \\text{air changes per hour}}\n$$\n\nThat is comfortably within the range needed for good indoor air quality, achieved with **no fan**, only a $7^\\circ\\text{C}$ temperature difference and a $4\\ \\text{m}$ shaft. This is exactly the leverage termites exploit: a modest temperature gap, applied over height through a well-shaped geometry, replaces mechanical work.\n\nBefore reading on, test your physical intuition about how each step in this calculation behaves:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Reasoning Through the Ventilation Calculation\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"The temperature difference is only 7 degrees C, producing a stack pressure of about 1.07 Pa. Is that pressure enough to do useful ventilation work?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"No, 1 Pa is far too small to move any air\",\"Yes, if the flow path has low resistance, ~1 Pa can drive steady airflow\",\"Only if a fan boosts it first\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"A single pascal is tiny compared to a fan (hundreds of Pa), but through a low-resistance, well-shaped path it sustains continuous flow. The mound relies on minimizing resistance, not maximizing pressure.\"},{\"prompt\":\"If you doubled the open vent area A while keeping h, Ti, and To fixed, what happens to the volumetric flow Q?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Q stays the same\",\"Q roughly doubles\",\"Q quadruples\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Stack pressure dP and therefore speed v depend on height and temperature, not on A. So Q = vA scales linearly with area: doubling A roughly doubles Q (until the larger opening starts changing the effective resistance).\"},{\"reveal\":\"Takeaway: speed v comes from the buoyancy story (h and the temperature ratio), while flow Q is then set by the vent area. Designers tune them separately: height and temperature for pressure, area for throughput.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n> **Think About It:** If the shaft height doubled to $8\\ \\text{m}$, $\\Delta P$ would double to about $2.13\\ \\text{Pa}$, and since $v \\propto \\sqrt{\\Delta P}$, the flow speed would rise only by a factor of $\\sqrt{2} \\approx 1.41$, to about $1.23\\ \\text{m/s}$. Height helps, but with diminishing returns.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Example 2: Thermal Mass as a Battery for Coolth\n\n**Problem.** A passively cooled building stores nighttime coolness in a concrete floor slab measuring $100\\ \\text{m}^2$ in area and $0.20\\ \\text{m}$ thick. Concrete has density $\\rho = 2400\\ \\text{kg/m}^3$ and specific heat $c = 880\\ \\text{J/(kg\u00b7K)}$. If night ventilation cools the slab by $\\Delta T = 6\\ \\text{K}$, how much thermal energy can the slab absorb from the building the next day?\n\n**Step 1: Find the slab mass.**\n\n$$\nm = \\rho V = 2400 \\times (100 \\times 0.20) = 2400 \\times 20 = 48{,}000\\ \\text{kg}\n$$\n\n**Step 2: Apply the sensible-heat storage relation.**\n\n$$\nE = m\\,c\\,\\Delta T = (48{,}000)(880)(6) = 2.534 \\times 10^{8}\\ \\text{J}\n$$\n\n**Step 3: Convert to practical units.**\n\n$$\nE = \\frac{2.534 \\times 10^{8}}{3.6 \\times 10^{6}} = \\boxed{70.4\\ \\text{kWh}}\n$$\n\nThat stored \"coolth\" can offset a substantial fraction of a day's cooling load before any active system is needed. This is the engineering meaning of **thermal buffering**: the slab behaves like a thermal flywheel, charged at night and discharged during the day, smoothing the temperature swing the occupants feel. The termite mound's thick walls and surrounding soil do the same job through their own large thermal mass.\n\nSize the \"coolth battery\" yourself. The explorer computes slab mass, stored energy, and the equivalent in kWh as you vary the slab geometry, the night-charging temperature drop, and the material properties.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Thermal-Mass Coolth Battery Sizer\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"Area\",\"label\":\"Slab area\",\"min\":20,\"max\":300,\"step\":10,\"unit\":\"m^2\",\"default\":100},{\"name\":\"thk\",\"label\":\"Slab thickness\",\"min\":0.05,\"max\":0.5,\"step\":0.01,\"unit\":\"m\",\"default\":0.2},{\"name\":\"dT\",\"label\":\"Night-charging temp drop\",\"min\":1,\"max\":12,\"step\":0.5,\"unit\":\"K\",\"default\":6},{\"name\":\"rho\",\"label\":\"Material density\",\"min\":600,\"max\":2600,\"step\":50,\"unit\":\"kg/m^3\",\"default\":2400},{\"name\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Specific heat\",\"min\":800,\"max\":1200,\"step\":10,\"unit\":\"J/(kg*K)\",\"default\":880}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Slab mass m\",\"unit\":\"kg\",\"expr\":\"rho * Area * thk\"},{\"label\":\"Stored energy E\",\"unit\":\"J\",\"expr\":\"rho * Area * thk * c * dT\"},{\"label\":\"Stored energy\",\"unit\":\"kWh\",\"expr\":\"rho * Area * thk * c * dT / 3600000\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"thk\",\"output\":2}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\n---\n\n## Translating Biology into Architecture\n\nArchitects turned to termite-inspired cooling because many hot climates face the same constraint: mechanical HVAC is expensive, energy-intensive, and often poorly matched to local conditions.\n\nThe **Eastgate Centre** in Harare, Zimbabwe is the classic case. Its design borrows from termite-inspired passive ventilation logic:\n\n- Massive walls and floor slabs store and release heat slowly (the thermal-mass principle of Worked Example 2).\n- Tall internal flues let warm air rise and exit at roof level (the stack effect of Worked Example 1).\n- Night air is drawn through the structure to \"charge\" the thermal mass with coolness.\n- The building relies on form and thermal behavior first, machinery second; low-power fans assist circulation rather than provide the primary cooling.\n\nThe reported result is striking: the building uses on the order of **10 percent of the cooling energy** of a conventionally air-conditioned building of similar size, with correspondingly lower running costs. The lesson is not \"copy the mound shape exactly.\" The lesson is to identify the **underlying mechanism**:\n\n- What heat source is driving the system?\n- Where can hot air safely leave?\n- Where can cooler air enter with minimal energy cost?\n- Which materials slow down thermal swings?\n\nThat is the real biomimetic move: copying the principle, not the silhouette.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"The Eastgate Centre does not look like a termite mound, yet it is called termite-inspired. In your own words, explain which underlying mechanisms it copied from the mound and why copying the mechanism (rather than the mound shape) is what makes it work.\" answer=\"Eastgate copied two functional mechanisms, not the appearance. First, the stack effect: tall internal flues let warm interior air rise and exhaust at roof level, drawing cooler air in low, which moves air with little or no fan power. Second, thermal mass: massive walls and slabs absorb heat slowly and are charged with coolness by night ventilation, then discharge it during the day to smooth temperature swings. Copying the mechanism transfers because the physics (buoyancy-driven flow and sensible-heat storage) works at building scale; copying the literal mound silhouette would transfer geometry that was tuned to a colony's needs, not a building's loads, occupancy, or climate.\"></self-explain>\n\n## A Problem-to-Biology Translation Example\n\nOne of the hardest parts of biologically inspired design is translating an engineering problem into language that can actually retrieve useful biological analogies.\n\nFor termite-inspired cooling, that translation might look like this:\n\n| Engineering Need | Biology-Oriented Search Language |\n| --- | --- |\n| Reduce HVAC demand | passive thermal regulation |\n| Move warm air without large fans | pressure-driven ventilation |\n| Limit indoor temperature swings | thermal buffering |\n| Exchange air through many paths | distributed vent network |\n\nThis translation layer matters because engineers and biologists often describe the same phenomenon with different vocabularies. Function-based search and engineering-to-biology thesauri help bridge that gap.\n\n## What an AskNature-Style Entry Would Capture\n\nIf this example were stored in a functionally indexed system such as AskNature, the most useful entry would not simply be labeled \"termite mound.\" It would likely be indexed by functions such as:\n\n- regulate temperature,\n- move fluid passively,\n- buffer environmental variation,\n- and distribute exchange through many small pathways.\n\n| Design Workflow Lens | Termite-Cooling Example |\n| --- | --- |\n| Engineering Problem | Regulate temperature with low external energy input |\n| Biological Strategy | Distributed venting plus thermal buffering |\n| Transfer Rule | Coordinate airflow paths, heat storage, and environmental exchange |\n| Validation Check | Re-test performance against local climate, occupancy, and maintenance constraints |\n\nThat style of indexing supports problem-driven retrieval. A greenhouse engineer, battery-pack designer, and architect may all discover the same biological strategy from different industries because the function is shared.\n\nWork through the design dilemma below. Each choice commits you to a tradeoff that the termite-cooling principles predict.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Cooling a Desert Research Building\",\"start\":\"start\",\"nodes\":{\"start\":{\"prompt\":\"Your team must cool a hot, dry-climate research building with a large daytime cooling load and a big day-to-night temperature swing. Where do you begin?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Install a large central HVAC chiller and stop there\",\"next\":\"hvac\",\"feedback\":\"This works but ignores the free leverage the climate offers, leading to high energy cost.\"},{\"label\":\"Lead with passive strategies and add mechanical backup only where needed\",\"next\":\"passive\",\"feedback\":\"This mirrors the termite logic: let the structure do part of the work first.\"}]},\"hvac\":{\"prompt\":\"Energy bills are very high. You revisit the climate data and see a 15 degree C day-to-night swing. What termite principle were you ignoring?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Thermal mass charged by cool night air\",\"next\":\"mass\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly. A large diurnal swing is free coolth waiting to be stored.\"},{\"label\":\"Nothing, big swings cannot help\",\"next\":\"hvac\",\"feedback\":\"Not so. A large swing is precisely what makes night-charged thermal mass effective.\"}]},\"passive\":{\"prompt\":\"You add thermal mass and stack-effect flues. The lab equipment still needs a tight, stable temperature band. What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Trust passive cooling to hold the band exactly on its own\",\"next\":\"fail\",\"feedback\":\"Passive cooling smooths swings but cannot guarantee a narrow setpoint under high internal loads.\"},{\"label\":\"Use passive cooling for the base load and a small active system to trim the band\",\"next\":\"hybrid\",\"feedback\":\"Right. The professional question is how much load passive removes before active systems handle the remainder.\"}]},\"mass\":{\"prompt\":\"You now night-ventilate to charge a concrete slab. The slab discharges coolth through the day. What did the mound teach you?\",\"feedback\":\"Thermal mass acts as a coolth battery (E = m c dT). A large diurnal swing makes that battery cheap to charge. Combine it with stack-effect airflow for a full passive base.\",\"choices\":[]},\"hybrid\":{\"prompt\":\"Final design: night-charged thermal mass, stack-effect flues, and a trimmed active system for the tight band the lab needs.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the termite lesson applied honestly: passive design removes most of the load through geometry and material, and a small active system covers what buoyancy and mass cannot.\",\"choices\":[]},\"fail\":{\"prompt\":\"The sensitive equipment drifts outside its band on the hottest afternoons.\",\"feedback\":\"Passive systems are powerful but not magic. Under high internal loads, a pascal or two of buoyancy pressure cannot move enough air. Add modest mechanical backup for the tight setpoint.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Passive Cooling Strategy\" context=\"A design team must reduce cooling costs for a new university research building in a desert climate while keeping sensitive lab equipment within a narrow temperature range.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n---\n\n## Design Heuristics You Can Reuse\n\nWhen you want to apply termite-inspired regulation to engineering or architecture, start with these heuristics:\n\n1. **Use mass to stabilize, not just structure.** Heavy materials can function as thermal storage; size them against the daily load, as in Worked Example 2.\n2. **Create vertical pathways for hot air escape.** If heat cannot leave, passive cooling collapses. Maximize the effective stack height $h$.\n3. **Design intake and exhaust together.** A vent without a pressure story is just a hole; the intake-to-exhaust height sets $\\Delta P$.\n4. **Treat environmental cycles as assets.** Cool night air, shade, and wind can all become part of the system.\n5. **Avoid over-centralizing the solution.** Distributed pathways often make the system more resilient.\n\nThese heuristics work beyond buildings. The same logic can inform battery enclosures, server rooms, greenhouse control, and even packaging for temperature-sensitive materials.\n\n---\n\n## Failure Modes and Tradeoffs\n\nPassive systems are powerful, but they are not magic.\n\n- If climate conditions are too humid, airflow alone may not solve comfort or preservation problems.\n- If internal heat loads are extremely high, the buoyancy pressure (often only a pascal or two) cannot move enough air, and mechanical backup is required.\n- If vents are placed poorly, the system can short-circuit, moving air in ways that bypass the protected zone.\n- If thermal mass is too large or too poorly insulated, the system may respond too slowly when conditions change.\n\nSo the professional question is never \"Can passive design replace all HVAC forever?\" It is \"How much of the load can passive design remove before active systems are required?\"\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Stack pressure | $\\Delta P = \\rho_o\\, g\\, h\\, \\dfrac{T_i - T_o}{T_i}$ |\n| Vent airflow speed | $v = C_d \\sqrt{2\\,\\Delta P / \\rho}$ |\n| Volumetric flow | $Q = vA$ |\n| Air changes per hour | $\\text{ACH} = Q / V$ |\n| Thermal-mass storage | $E = m\\,c\\,\\Delta T$ |\n\n### Key Principles\n\n- The mound regulates temperature through **geometry and material**, not machinery.\n- The stack effect converts a small temperature difference, applied over height, into steady airflow.\n- Thermal mass acts as a buffer (a \"coolth battery\") that smooths daily temperature swings.\n- Distributed vents share the exchange load and add resilience.\n- Biomimicry means copying the **function and mechanism**, not the literal shape.\n\nMap how the core concepts of this section connect, from the physical mechanism through to the design discipline that exploits it:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"stack_effect\",\"thermal_mass\",\"passive_cooling\",\"biomimetic_architecture\"]'></concept-map>\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Which statement best captures the engineering lesson of the termite mound?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Efficient cooling mainly comes from building the tallest possible chimney.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"The mound works because its geometry, vents, and thermal mass create pressure-driven airflow and slow temperature swings.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"The mound stays cool because termites actively pump air through the walls like mechanical fans.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. The key idea is not a single feature but the interaction of thermal mass, vent placement, and pressure-driven airflow. The mound behaves like a passive control system.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn the next section we extend this passive-regulation thinking from a single structure to **adaptive envelopes**: surfaces and skins that change their behavior in response to sun, wind, and humidity, taking the termite's static geometry one step further toward responsive design.\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "bio_stack_effect_core", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "stack_effect", "stem": "What is the stack effect in termite-inspired ventilation design?", "options": ["Heat-driven air movement caused by density differences between warmer and cooler air", "A method for increasing concrete weight in foundations", "A purely electrical cooling mechanism", "A strategy for eliminating all airflow through a structure"], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "The stack effect relies on buoyancy